<article class="env-remark" id="256588">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/256588">Remark <span data-tag="256588">6.5.4.8</span>.</a> A <em>point</em>point!of an $\infty $-topos of an $\infty $-topos $\calX $ is a geometric morphism $p_{\ast }: \SSet \rightarrow \calX $, where $\SSet $ denotes the $\infty $-category of spaces (which is a final object of $\RGeom $, in virtue of Proposition <a href="/tag/A780C6" data-tag="A780C6">6.3.4.1</a>). We say that $\calX $ has <em>enough points</em>enough points if, for every morphism $f: X \rightarrow Y$ in $\calX $ having the property that $p^{\ast }(f)$ is an equivalence for <em>every</em> point $p$ of $\calX $, $f$ is itself an equivalence in $\calX $. If $f$ is $\infty $-connective, then every stalk $p^{\ast }(f)$ is $\infty $-connective, hence an equivalence by Whitehead's theorem. Consequently, if $\calX $ has enough points, then it is hypercomplete. </p>
  <p>In classical topos theory, Deligne's version of the Gödel completeness theorem (see <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/where">where</a>]</span>) asserts that every coherent topos has enough points. Counterexample <a href="/tag/A2ABF2" data-tag="A2ABF2">6.5.4.6</a> shows that there exist coherent topological spaces with $\Shv (X)^{\wedge } \neq \Shv (X)$, so that $\Shv (X)$ does not necessarily have enough points. Consequently, Deligne's theorem does not hold in the $\infty $-categorical context. </p>
</article>